    "recommendations": "Reducing the continuity of vegetation around the structure, particularly the brush observed in close proximity to the building, could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce opportunities for fire to reach the structure. Thinning or removing brush immediately adjacent to the structure and increasing separation between scattered trees, shrubs, and other vegetation within the near-structure zone may reduce fuel continuity across the property. Managing vegetation along the length of the driveway would support firefighter access and improve conditions for both ingress during suppression operations and egress during evacuation. Where scattered trees are present near the structure, maintaining vertical separation between lower branches and surface vegetation could further reduce fuel continuity. The rock outcrop present on the property represents a natural fuel break and its surroundings could be kept clear of encroaching brush to preserve and extend that benefit. Continued attention to vegetation management within the immediate structure perimeter and along the access route would support long-term wildfire resilience."
